{"path": "frontier/iterations/i66-dispersive-equation-discovery.md", "sha256": "92b819a856dd383bd758b1486cb46e02d610ade7baf2cb5dd1cd071851d7befe", "bytes": 35793, "start": 1, "end": 78, "total_lines": 353, "passage_sha256": "a8ac8ba38a7b77810674af87da3b3797b64c3d9cacf18e437a7714d8ede01aa4", "ending": "The actual nearest primary operations are the exponential Lyapunov–Perron construction in Buck–Carballeira–Gómez-Serrano–Shi [2609.23685v1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.23685v1), Proposition 4.8; Li's symmetry-parameter matching and conditional open sets in [2304.02078v2](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.02078v2), Theorems 1.4–1.5 and §6.3; and periodic cutoff/finite-codimension constructions in Cao-Labora–Gómez-Serrano–Shi–Staffilani [2410.04532v1](https://arxiv.org/html/2410.04532v1), Theorem 1.1. The first treats specific pure-power whole-space equations, the second focusing pure powers, and the third a different scaling in dimension eight. Donninger–Wallauch's [2006.04600v1](https://arxiv.org/html/2006.04600v1) is a genuine robustness antecedent, but for a semilinear wave equation; its §1.1, equation (1.5), requires a perturbing power q<p. For nonzero κ, the magnitude `r^p/log(2+r²)` is not O(1+r^q) for any fixed q<p, so even that specific growth interface would not cover this perturbation. None of these inspected statements is (4)'s periodic defocusing theorem. This is a bounded comparison, not a certificate that every perturbative NLS paper has been excluded.", "convention": "UTF-8 splitlines; joined LF; no appended LF"}
